translated_content:
The former minister under General Mohamed Siyaad Barre’s regime didn’t have a spotless past—but receiving a gold medal for national service and education reform still carries major weight, and it’s vital that he now backs the new government.

Mr. Mudane Aden Mohamed Ali, who once served under Barre as Minister of Sports, Youth & Culture; Minister of Education & Rehabilitation; and Minister of Health & Social Affairs, has publicly thrown his support behind President Mohamed Abdullahi Mohamed (Farmaajo)’s new administration. The new government—including President Farmaajo, Prime Minister Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, and the entire cabinet—has welcomed his endorsement.

President Farmaajo’s endorsement:

Prime Minister Hassan Sheikh Mohamud’s endorsement:

Who was the former minister under Mohamed Siyaad?

Born in 1939, Mudane completed primary school locally and graduated from university in Mogadishu in 1958. His key roles include:

1965: Head of the National Teachers’ Union;

1967–1969: Chairman of the Somali Youth League;

1971: Head of Education for Banaadir Region;

1974: Minister of Sports, Youth & Culture;

1976: Minister of Education & Rehabilitation;

1982: Head of External Affairs at the Somali National Sports Council;

1984: Minister of Health & Social Affairs;

1988: Head of Administration & Oversight at the Somali National Sports Council;

1976–1991: Member of the Central Committee of the Somali National Sports Council.

For his decades of service, Mudane was awarded the 10th Anniversary Gold Medal by the Somali National Sports Council—alongside Mohamed Cali Samantar, who received the same honor for his work in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.

Mudane isn’t just a veteran bureaucrat—he’s a national conscience who openly condemned the Barre regime’s crimes and now backs Farmaajo to rescue Somalia.

He was personally received by President Farmaajo, PM Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, and the late national icon Mohamed Sh. Cusmaan Jawaari, and reaffirmed his commitment to rebuilding the nation.
